.TH USERS 5
.SH NAME
users, groups, secrets \- the account files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/users
.br
.B /etc/groups
.br
.B /etc/secrets
.SH DESCRIPTION
The accounts of SIEOS live in three text files: one record per line, fields
separated by a tab;
.B #
starts a comment.
Only the accounts server,
.BR auth (8),
changes them, and only it reads
.BR /etc/secrets .
Each change is written to FILE.new and renamed over FILE in one step, so a
crash leaves the old file or the new one, never a mix.
.TP
.B /etc/users
(mode 0644) name, uid, gid, home, shell, full name.
.TP
.B /etc/groups
(mode 0644) name, gid, members (comma-separated names).
.TP
.B /etc/secrets
(mode 0600) name, method
.RB ( argon2id ),
memory (KiB), passes, lanes, salt (hex), hash (hex).
.PP
uid 0 is root.
Users get uids from 1000 up; each has a group of its own (the same number).
Users created after the first start are also members of
.B users
(gid 100).
.PP
A password is never stored, only its Argon2id hash: a 16-byte random salt,
a 32-byte result, 16 MiB of memory, 24 passes, 1 lane.
The parameters are stored with each hash, so they can change later without
breaking older passwords.
.PP
The rules auth checks: a login name of 1 to 31 characters, lower-case
letters, digits,
.B _
or
.BR \- ,
starting with a letter or
.BR _ ,
not
.BR root ;
a full name of up to 63 characters, no tab; a password of at least 8
characters, not the account's name.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
alice	1000	1000	/home/alice	/bin/sh	Alice Example
users	100	alice,bob
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR passwd (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR useradd (8),
.BR auth (8),
.BR security (7)
